00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.280
So when we

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.280
kind of think about MCP, I'll just kind of

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.680
quickly kind of introduce what MCP is.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.680
I'm sure most
people are already fairly familiar, but

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.720
the MCP protocol

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.280
by its design
was designed to be bidirectional.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:31.560
Okay.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.440
So the kind of the basic idea was
that you would have MCP clients

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.480
and they would connect to MCP servers,

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.800
and they'd almost been this kind of peer
to peer relationship,

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.640
where the pairings of them
would work together to to kind of modify

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.120
the context of the,
of what you were using.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:49.480
Okay.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:53.320
So MCP clients can obviously use tools
and prompts and so on.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.200
And the idea being that MCP servers
could also make requests to the client

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.560
and use things like the LM attached
or the file system attached.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:07.160
So again,
quite similar to the way that LSP

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.280
servers work, work with Ides

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:12.760
now kind

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.760
of almost famously when it was launched,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.400
MCP launched with two transports
local transport, which

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.360
which runs in normally the same processes
as your application

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.920
can access the file system and so on,
and also a remote transport called SSE.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.280
But there was no authentication at all.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.800
Shortly afterwards,

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.800
it was kind of quite clear
that the SAC transport

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.120
was pretty difficult to work with
in that in that way.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.440
So a new transport

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.400
was launched called stream of HTTP
to make it a bit easier to deploy.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.520
And at that time, authentication

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.320
was also added, with the MCP server

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.520
meant to be acting as an authorization
server, which again turned out to be

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.560
slightly too complicated,
and so ultimately a better design to use.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.040
A water resource server
was then introduced.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.320
And that's kind of where we 
where we kicked off.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.800
So at launch
time for us about 12 months ago,

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.400
we were still at the point
where we weren't sure

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.760
whether to use the SSE transport

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.160
or how to tackle their walls,
but we kind of just went right.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.680
We're doing the new stuff

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:22.200
now when

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.680
when we kind of first launch 
