But polite to talk to me first.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.040
So when it comes to kind

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.480
of understanding activity on the MCP
server, there's kind of a few things

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:28.000
to think about if you're kind of
running it in production.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.040
And the first one is quite often
you might think that initializations

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:35.280
might be quite a good,
a good thing to to track.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:40.000
And it's not really the case because while
in some clients, initializations

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.880
will kind of mean that you've got an
ambient installation, because every time

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:47.840
someone opens their tool,
it connects to our server.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:52.200
In other cases,
clients will cache and only initialize

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.560
when they make it all call.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:58.160
We also tend to find a lot of
clients are not necessarily as stable

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.080
as they should be, and will sometimes
get into runaway loops where they kind of

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:06.440
just carry on initializing
and causing a bit of trouble.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.600
The other one, which we kind of look at is
tool calls.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:13.640
Now, often people will think, well,
actually, you know, we're doing better

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:14.760
if we've got more tool calls.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.920
And that's not necessarily the case,
because if you get lots of repeated

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.480
tool calls,
that may simply mean that your tool design

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:23.960
is causing confusion
to the downstream agent.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:26.960
And again, someone using

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:30.680
this through an through an interactive
chatter system that's going to be doing

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:33.120
something quite different
to what they would be doing

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.680
if they were using it
interactively through, say, ChatGPT.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:40.480
So what we tend

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.640
to or what I tend to look at is session
conversion.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.760
So how many clients connect
and then make at least one tool call.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.760
And that's quite, quite an interesting
count for me on the right.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.280
Again, the
the session conversion efficiency

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.280
kind of shows some of the main clients
that attached to us,

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.280
the ones at the top will connect
and make a tool call.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.440
So OpenMCP: 96.2% of the time.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.640
No surprise, it's the agent builder
that does that because of the nature

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:07.720
of how it's built.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.280
MCP remote fallback test.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.480
Of course, it's a fallback test.
It never makes it all call.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.240
So more efficiency
isn't necessarily better because if

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.240
you've incorrectly cached it at all list,

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.560
you're not going to be making a call
to the MCP server itself.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.080
So these are some quite
difficult things to balance

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:29.160
when kind
of trying to understand activity.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:32.680
But if we look at the last few weeks,

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.600
Codex is a very efficient client

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:36.840
and is more and more
people have kind of gone on to Codex.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.600
One thing I've actually seen here

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:42.640
is, is the the conversion
rate has kind of gone up.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.040
And again, we can see that
there's still an increasing amount

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.040
of useful session to talk or conversion

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.040
traffic.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:55.280
We also publish

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:58.960
an data set, which contains

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.160
a kind of list of all of the clients
that we've seen and their capabilities.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:07.320
So if you kind of want to have a look
at what version

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:13.160
clients are at, what capabilities
or extensions they've got installed,

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.240
then that data set is a very,
very good place to start.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.960
So have a look, check it out because,

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.160
you know, probably be publishing
a bit more data through there fairly soon.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.800
Now in production,

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:33.200
the MCP
kind of gives us a couple of challenges.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:38.080
And the main challenge is comes back
from that initial design, right?

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.640
Which is that if I have a kind of a client
and servant pairing,

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:45.680
there's an assumption in the protocol
that the

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:50.320
the relationship
between them is stateful.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.840
So what's actually happening here is,
is that the client will make a request

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:01.760
to the server,
and the server says I've initialized.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.400
And then the client sends back
a notification and they've kind of hand

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:06.200
shaken.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.280
So we've already kind of two messages
from the client

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.280
to the server in before
we've actually established a session.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.200
After that, the client
then will normally check and say, right,

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:16.800
well give me the list of tools,

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:18.760
give me the list of prompts,
give me the list of resources.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:22.720
We're so we're now kind of we're at
four calls in before we've actually even

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:24.120
we've actually even serviced anything.

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:25.880
And then finally we'll get a tool call

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.160
in the information.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.800
We'll go back to the client.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.800
Now again kind of typically

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:38.080
what what we'll see in these
kind of scenarios is if we've got state

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:41.080
at both the client and the server,
that starts to get rather

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.400
rather tricky to manage.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.360
And it's also very, very chatty.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:52.080
So I kind of ran this
I think it was on Sunday morning.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.760
This number kind of varies a bit based on
the mix of clients we've recently seen.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.440
But at the moment, for every 10 million
protocol messages that we get.

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.520
So these are the kind of MCP
protocol methods and calls, 1.2

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:09.080
million of them are initialize events,
which can may be interesting,

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:11.960
but actually only 62,000 of them at all
calls.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:16.880
So the kind of overhead of managing
that stateful connection

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:21.440
is, is quite high from
from a protocol level.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:26.680
Now there is a kind of semi documented

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:29.880
undocumented mode
where you can kind of run MCP stateless.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.000
It's a bit ambiguous, but if you do that,

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.040
you lose any ability
to kind of capture analytics.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.560
So some of the challenges
then we have with with stateful.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.640
So one is that ambiguity
around, around the protocol,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.720
the use of elicitation and sampling,
if you've got server

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:52.200
initiated requests, mean
that you need to maintain an open channel.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:52.480
Okay.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.960
So one of the things that we were quite
keen on trying when we first

00:12:55.960 --> 00:13:00.640
launched was to send what are called
tool list change notifications.

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.120
So the MCP server, if it wants to offer
different tools, will notify the client.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.440
And the client can then offer them.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.960
But actually in practice,
that's a very, very difficult thing to do,

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:10.080
especially on a public server.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:13.840
As I mentioned, session
state and understanding

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.120
that is not quite precisely
defined enough in the protocol.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.200
So a standard IO server,
you kind of just connect,

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.600
you've got a process and you talk to it
a remote server,

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:29.160
you have an MCP session ID,
it doesn't necessarily match up

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.440
an operationally having sticky sessions
and load balancers,

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.880
which need to inspect the traffic

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.960
to send it to the right server, means
that doing server upgrades, resilience,

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.360
or kind of standard scaling
practice becomes very, very difficult.

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:48.080
The other challenge as well
is SSE connection

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:52.200
and cut off times make a lot of that
quite, quite difficult as well.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:54.840
So those are some of the

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:58.080
challenges
and kind of what we see in production. So.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.320
It's been probably
about over the last ten months

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:08.120
then been working with the MCP transports
working group.

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:12.600
We've got people from Google
and Microsoft and everybody

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.440
contributing to that kind
of sharing the production data,

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.560
sharing our experience
of how to actually deploy this.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:24.120
And we now in the new release
candidate specification, have some fairly

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:30.080
big changes to the protocol to actually
make this easier and simpler to deploy.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:34.240
And I think, you know, to get
get a lot more, a lot more value out of.

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:38.720
So kind of the first thing
to kind of think about then is this, that

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:41.840
